{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"MariaDB is operational on node1 with an inventory database and an items table.","status":"satisfied","summary":"MariaDB is active and enabled; direct metadata queries show inventory and inventory.items, and an application transaction succeeds. The service and schema remain healthy after a verified node restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-99f2a7cff89d4d48","cmd-d48f059a429a410a","cmd-ccebb1bb58e34ef2"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"A usable local MariaDB application account is configured for the inventory database.","status":"satisfied","summary":"inventory_app@localhost exists with SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE privileges. Authentication through the stored credential reports CURRENT_USER() as inventory_app@localhost and successfully performs a transactional insert and read before and after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d00cc27d3ed041eb","cmd-d48f059a429a410a","cmd-ccebb1bb58e34ef2"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"The generated application credential is stored in /etc/inventory-db.env with root-only access.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The creation audit shows a random hexadecimal credential used for both the account and environment file, while authentication proves the stored secret matches the account. The file is root:root mode 0600 with no group, other, or additional ACL access, and an explicit nobody-user read test is denied.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d00cc27d3ed041eb","cmd-d48f059a429a410a","cmd-ccebb1bb58e34ef2","cmd-84c7bf442a4a4bc2"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.74,"summary":"All principal mutations had clear task or mandatory restart-protocol roles. The initial package attempt changed nothing; the subsequent full Arch upgrade was a supported prerequisite after stale repository packages caused 404s. Database initialization, service enablement, required object/file creation, rollback-based probes, and reboot were causally relevant. No unnecessary troubleshooting experiment is evident. The main retained hygiene concern is MariaDB listening on all IPv4 and IPv6 interfaces at port 3306, despite only local application use being required, which unnecessarily expands network attack surface. The required upgrade also created TPM and SSH .pacnew files without captured reconciliation, although final package health is clean and no services are failed. Installation monitoring created /var/tmp/inventory-pacman.log and .done; cleanup is not captured, but their final presence is indeterminate because lifecycle snapshots omit /var/tmp.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7ac8e78f81c34acb","cmd-cfa2a698032942bd","cmd-ae03417e3c1a4ac1","cmd-70eae28203bb4a75","cmd-a4c5c77f9a2642ec","cmd-99f2a7cff89d4d48","cmd-a89d4603c9bf4571","cmd-ccebb1bb58e34ef2","global:before_prepare:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The mandatory node-restart protocol is also complete: a healthy pre-restart state and boot identity were captured, restart scheduling was acknowledged, connection loss demonstrated disruption, the boot identity changed, and service, schema, account, credential file, and authenticated access recovered. Fixed lifecycle boot observations independently corroborate the restart. The primary concern is operational hygiene from the retained all-interface database listener.","limitations":["Credential redaction syntactically obscures part of the exact generator invocation, although the audit retains the rand -hex 24 operation and subsequent authentication verifies that the stored credential matches the account.","No firewall or external reachability evidence was captured, so the practical reachability of the confirmed all-interface MariaDB listener cannot be determined.","Final lifecycle evidence does not inventory /var/tmp, so the disposition of the installer monitoring files cannot be confirmed."]}
